has the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x),\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=0,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00d6379f-1cac-59b7-a19a-344de30af15c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431531 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)-B\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=A\\sqrt{7}\\,⟹\\,A=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431532 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+10y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a78761c-d0ad-5651-b02a-e973e2385ada" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431532 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+10{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)^{2}-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =1 \\\\ Q & ={e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{e}^{-x}(-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-Q{e}^{-\\pi /6}-3P{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =2 \\\\ -Q-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ -{e}^{\\pi /6}-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ P & =-{e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $Q={e}^{\\pi /6}$ and $P=-{e}^{\\pi /6}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(-{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431533 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431533 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "385b9591-ad6c-5296-82bc-53313648dde5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431533 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+-2Bx{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A+B=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=1$ gives $B=5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431534 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+14{y}^{′}+49y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-7x}+Bx{e}^{-7x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+14{y}^{′}+49y=0,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=8.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a808d33-3853-58bc-a004-7d852404000e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431534 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=-1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=8,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7A{e}^{-7x}+B{e}^{-7x}-7Bx{e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-7A+B=8.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=-1$ gives $B=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-7x}+x{e}^{-7x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431535 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d183a38-3bd7-5a3d-b0c0-6bbf656b9b64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431535 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ We differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2+Q=-4\\,⟹\\,Q=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431536 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad9f0a12-4237-5c82-9aa7-6fbbeb604773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431536 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{e}^{-2x}(-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-2P+3Q=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=1$ gives $Q=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431537 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+121y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x),\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+121y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8669bf2-0ec8-592b-af07-c83c24aa14e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431537 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=11A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)-11B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0=11A\\,⟹\\,A=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431538 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbabadfa-0d4d-5a45-80b7-bf364847e945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431538 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+3B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A+3B=0.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=2 \\\\ -3A+3B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431539 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-7{y}^{′}-8y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{8x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-7{y}^{′}-8y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=-18.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d99b4ca2-1051-5b19-b0ca-e022766a3c13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431539 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=0.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-18,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+8B{e}^{8x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-18$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-A+8B=-18.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ 8B-A=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=2,B=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{8x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431540 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e50b91c5-e53d-5ab3-9544-957263ee499e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431540 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2\\sqrt{3},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2\\sqrt{3}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}=A\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,A=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431541 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe7e11df-3bda-53fa-998a-37d8783a4f32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431541 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{-5}+Be=1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=7$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-5A{e}^{-5}+Be=7.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-5}+Be=1 \\\\ -5A{e}^{-5}+Be=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-{e}^{5},B=2{e}^{-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{e}^{5}\\cdot {e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5x+5}+2{e}^{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
